Folks,

OSF is currently determining the form of the DCE documentation set for the
1.1 release.  Because of the increasing size of the programming
documentation, because of concerns about appropriate audiences, and because
of licensee packaging issues we are considering a division of the docs as
follows:

	1) We are eliminating the User's Guide and moving the infromation
	   into the Administration Guide and Administration Referenc

	2) We are moving all commands, including the IDL compiler command
	   into the Admin Reference and renaming it the "Command Reference".

	3) We are creating a new book called the "Problem Determination
	   Guide" which will contain as detailed as possible information
	   about all DCE error returns and messages.

	4) We are separating the DFS administration information into
	   either a DFS Administration Guide and Reference (one volume) or
	   A DFS Administration Guide and a DFS Administration Reference.

 	5) We are considering similar changes for GDS.  There will be a
	   GDS Administration volume.  We are deciding whether to put the
	   reference documentation in this volume as well as the guide
	   material.  (We would NOT do a GDS Administration reference-only
	   volume, as this book would be too slim).

	6) We are dividing the Application Development Guide into three
	   units: an "Introduction and Style Guide" (most of which will
	   be new material), a "Core" volume and a "Directory Services"
	   volume.

We are considering a number of issues in makiog these decisions, including
book page counts, the number of volumes in the DCE doc set (conceivably as
high as 12 books at 1.1), our licensees' productization plans, the fact
that increasing the number of volumes increases the end-user costs, and the
different audiences/uses for the guide and reference documentation.  To
help us complete our decision making process, we would appreciate your your
feedback on the following:

Are you a direct OSF licensee?

Do you license DCE from another organization (Digital, HP, IBM, Transarc
....)?


Do you use DFS?

Would you prefer to have the DFS administration documentation:

	1) In a DFS Administration Guide and Reference as a single volume
	   (about 800 pages).

	2) In two separate DFS volumes, Guide and a Reference (each about
	   400 pages long).

What are your reasons for this preference?


Do you use GDS?

Would you prefer to have the GDS administration documentation:

	1) With the Guide material in a GDS Administrator's Guide
	   and the Reference material in the DCE Command Reference

	2) With all the material in a single GDS Administrator's
	   Guide and Reference?

What are your reasons for this preference?


We are writing a new "DCE Style Guide" that will add at least 200 pages to
the Application Developer's Guide, so we MUST divide this book into at
least two volumes.  Would you prefer to see:

	1) Three volumes: and Introduction and Style Guide (~250-350pp), 
	   a Core Programming Guide (~550-600pp), and a Directory Services
	   Programming Guide (~550-600 pp)

	2) Two volumes, one with the Introduction, Style Guide, and Core
	   programming documentation, (~800-950pp) and the second with
	   Directory Services documentation (~550-600pp).

What are your reasons for this preference?


We are also considering reorganizing the structure of the DCE Command
Reference.  This book will include the reference pages from the Admin
Reference and the User's Guide, and the few "section 1" reference pages
from the Programmer's Reference.  The current Administrator's Reference is
divided both by component (e.g., dce, thr, rpc, cds, dts, sec) and by
UNIX-style section numbers (1, 4, 7, 8).  This makes access to the document
on a purely alphabetical basis rather difficult.  We propose to reorder
the book so that it will still be divided into UNIX sections, but each
section would be in purely alphabetical order.

Should OSF reorganize the book as proposed?

If so:  What value do you think such a reorganization would add?

If not: 1) Why should we not do it?

Should we reorganize the book, but do it differently?
		   How?
		   Why?


I would appreciate receiving your responses by April 29.
--


				Hal Lichtin
				Open Software Foundation
				hal@osf.org  (617) 621-8809


