Yes and no.  If you execute setld -l <CDROM or RIS server>, *most* of the
contents of /usr/.smdb. will be restored.  This directory contains at least 3
files per subset (SUBSET.ctrl, SUBSET.inv, and SUBSET.scp).  These will be
restored for those subsets on the CDROM or in the RIS area.  If you loaded from
multiple RIS areas or CDROMs, you would need to run setld -l against them all.

When running setld -l, you'll get the software selection menu.  DON'T SELECT ANY
SUBSETS TO LOAD!!!

However, this won't restore the SUBSET.lk files.  These files are created by
setld when a subset has installed and was verified.  You'd need to load the
subset to get these made automatically.  Alternatively, if you know exactly what
has been loaded, you could create (touch) these .lk files manually for each
subset that you installed previously.

Better yet, restore /usr/.smdb. from the latest backup you did.  ;-)

Dave
Digital Equipment Corp.


