Careful Patrick, we don't want to confuse people ... 

SOM/DSOM is *not* TCP/IP-based. Cross-process within-machine messages are sent via shared 
memory IPC, while cross-machine messages are sent via a concrete implementation provided 
for an abstract communications class. The abstract sockets class' methods correspond to 
the sockets API. While the sockets interface is most closely associated with TCP/IP
calls, lots of transports can be mapped to sockets calls. We do this ourselves for the
concrete commumications class supporting IPX/SPX. AnyNet provides a sockets mapping into 
Netbios and SNA so SOM/DSOM uses AnyNet for Netbios and SNA support. If they want to,
users can write their own concrete communications class for other transports.

The point is that there is nothing about the DSOM architecture or implementation that is
inherently TCP/IP based - SOM/DSOM fully supports cross-machine communication using TCP/IP,
Netbios, IPX/SPX, and SNA.

regards,

frank campagnoni


