>Gotta love those unsubstantiated opinions.

Neat editing Rich!  To put back in the end of the line you so
carefully cut out: "This is an old argument."  More eloquent people
than I have detailed the problems with DCE as the interoperability
layer of CORBA, (if you are interested in this argument, read the old
postings from last fall and the interoperability papers submitted to
the OMG.) I don't have the time or the interest to go into this in
detail. However, I will summarize:

1) The development of ORB systems is in its early stages; it is
   to early to standardize on an implemenation, but not to early
   to standardize on an interface.

2) Standardizing on DCE as a transport implemention would stifle
   ORB innovation.

3) Four of the best ORB products currently available:
   IBM's DSOM, Ionia's Orbix, DEC's ObjectBroker, Sun's DOE are not
   based on DCE.  Two of these ORBs might have real problems switching
   to RPC, namely Orbix and DOE.

4) Examples of problems standardizing on DCE would cause are: requiring
   GUIDs for objects, requiring synchronous invocation only, disallowing
   operating system enhancements (see "Spring") to distributed object
   systems, etc.

Note that for all these reasons, interoperability via ONC would be
equally as bad (if not worse.)

>>Snort!  The only thing that DCE has going for it is that Microsoft
>>picked it as its technology base for Distributed OLE.  Otherwise, it
>>would be even less of a sucess than OSF/1 (though more than OSF DME).

>Glad to see you've been keeping that axe nice and sharp between posts.
	>/r$

Rich, you cut out the statement I was responding to, which was
roughly: "[DCE is the industry standard RPC system]."  This statement
is not true.  DCE may be the *best* of the older, RPC based
distributed systems.  I believe is better than ONC.  However, still in
mid-1994, ONC *is* the industry standard.

I strongly believe that the advantages of DCE over the current release
ONC are small enough that DCE would not replace ONC unless it had
Microsoft's backing.

I have the greatest respect for your technical abilities Rich, and all
you have given the Unix and networking communities over the years.
For this reason, I don't enjoy arguing in public with you.  However, I
am not going to let you or your cohorts make mistatements on this
subject.  Your colleague was wrong, and he got called on it.

sincere regards,
fletcher

