Fletcher Kittredge (fkittred@salt.fmrco.COM) wrote:

: 2) Standardizing on DCE as a transport implemention would stifle
:    ORB innovation.

Would it stifle it more, less, or the same as basing an ORB
implementation on top of, say, TCP or UDP, or (somehow) on top of some
generic transport-level API?

: 3) Four of the best ORB products currently available:
:    IBM's DSOM, Ionia's Orbix, DEC's ObjectBroker, Sun's DOE are not
:    based on DCE.  Two of these ORBs might have real problems switching
:    to RPC, namely Orbix and DOE.

This might say something about the implementations, as opposed to the
idea of using DCE underneath an ORB.

: 4) Examples of problems standardizing on DCE would cause are: requiring
:    GUIDs for objects, requiring synchronous invocation only, disallowing
:    operating system enhancements (see "Spring") to distributed object
:    systems, etc.

I don't see how using DCE under an ORB _requires_ using UUIDs (which
is what I assume you mean by GUIDs).  I also don't understand your
point about OS enhancements.  The synchonous invocation argument has
some merit since trying to implement an async call facility on top of
a synchronous one is hard and/or clumsy (although not impossible).  I
don't know that I'd call this fatal though (of course I happen to
think async invocation is not such a great abstraction--I prefer
synchronous invocation and threads).

I think it's easy to argue that just using DCE to implement an ORB
doesn't magically solve any problems or make things dramatically
easier or better.  But you seem to be arguing that it makes things
harder and/or worse, and I don't think you've made the case.  The way
I see it, DCE is a tool that raises the level of discussion and
specification WRT ORB implementations.  You get to define at least
some of the interop rules in terms of things like abstract (DCE) data
types and remote procedure calls.  Further, you have the option (not
the requirement) to use existing facilities--e.g., directory and
security services--rather than redesign and reimplement them.  This
seems like a useful thing.  An ORB implementation _could_ define its
own MAC, network, and transport level protocols too, but the virtues
of using Ethernet and TCP/IP seem to outweigh the costs (if any).  A
similar calculation needs to be made WRT DCE.

                -- Nat Mishkin
                   Atria Software, Inc.
