Fletcher, 

Let me paraphrase the originial questions posed on this subject:

1. Can one build a DCE implementation of CORBA? (has anyone done this?)
2. How might this be done? 
3. Why would anyone ever want to use DCE? (I'm exaggerating this question 
   for comedic effect :-)

The answer to 1 was *yes*. It's certainly possible to build CORBA over DCE. We
eventually all agreed there is one vendor (HP, right?) that is doing so. In
fact, if you read the joint proposal (again, OMG doc 94-3-5.ps) to the end you
will see a reference to a document entitled "HP ORB Plus Object (DCE) Messaging
Architecture". If you want to learn more about the current and future product
plans of your favorite system vendor (and express your disdain for DCE :-), I
suggest you contact them directly.  Again, I do not think that anyone made the
claim that this was the only way to build an ORB or that you should necessarily
use DCE, although I'm sure you can imagine some scenarios where it would
actually be desirable to actually use DCE rather than reinvent the wheel. To be
fair, I too can imagine some scenarios where I might not want to use DCE either
:-) See the answer to 3 below for more on where DCE might be useful.

The answer to 2 was not really complete but I suggested that one could map
CORBA/IDL to DCE/IDL and that one could read the joint submission to get some
idea of how this could be done (note: the joint submission is not product
literature but a joint specification of a possible CORBA-to-DCE mapping, as
yet unstandardized, but before OMG).

My answer to 3 was that DCE "could" form the basis for CORBA interoperability.
I was careful to point out that DCE was not the only way to achieve
interoperability and that there might be other interoperability protocols
(hopefully, a small number of them). You could have private interoperability
between ORBs (we allow for this), but wouldn't it be beneficial to have
one/several publically specified ones? For the record, the joint submission
allows one to negotiate a common protocol whatever it might be, public or
private, DCE-based or not. Yeah, we specified a CORBA-to-DCE mapping but this
does not preclude the specification of other common protocols.  

Here's an excerpt from the design goals section (of course, I suggest you read
the whole proposal sometime):

* Support Multiple Protocols

Given the vastly different architectures and implementations of
operating systems and communications mechanisms today, we realize that
it would be difficult (or impossible) to standardize on a single
interoperability protocol, even though there are likely to be a small
number of them. In our approach, we thus support the notion that there
may be several interoperability protocols.  We have chosen a tagged
approach to uniquely identify protocols and to allow an object to
support multiple protocols.

We propose a generic, high-level interoperability architecture which allows one
to negotiate and use an appropriate interoperability protocol.  We are using
the term "protocol" in a broad sense.  A protocol encompasses everything that
is necessary for CORBA implementations to communicate with each other,
including protocol specific object reference information, location mechanisms,
and lastly the communications protocol itself.

We chose to define interoperability solely based on the negotiation and use of
a common protocol.  We believe that a gateway solution cannot provide 
uniform and correct semantics across communication environments.
However, our design does not preclude the use of gateways.

* Fully Specify a Common Protocol for DCE

While providing a general framework for interoperability, we must also ensure
that there exists at least one common protocol in order to practically achieve
interoperability.  Because of the heterogeneous interoperability inherent in
OSF DCE, we have chosen it as the underlying layer of the protocol that we
describe.  The DCE Application Environment Specification (AES) specifies a
protocol that guarantees interoperability of conformant implementations.  In
addition, it is an established vendor neutral technology providing an
integrated set of distributed services with proven vendor implementations.  The
joint submitters of this proposal have proven through existing ORB
implementations, that DCE is a suitable platform for CORBA.

We firmly believe that the low-level details of how exactly a given protocol
can be used to achieve interoperability must be specified.  We provides a
complete CORBA to DCE mapping that allows a vendor to independently implement
an ORB over DCE and maintain interoperability with other DCE implementations.

Have fun,

Andy

