Excerpts from netnews.comp.unix.osf.misc: 22-Apr-94 DCE-LFS fileset
question Lisianne MARZANI@merle.a (750) 

> I understand that a LFS fileset can contain a subdirectory that actually is 
> a mount point for another fileset. 

Well, DCE DFS filesets can have ``mount points'' (really on-disk
junction names pointing to other filesets) stored in them, regardless of
whether the ``mount points'' are stored in DCE LFS filesets or other
DFS-exported filesets.  This is an interpretation made by the DCE DFS
cache manager. 

> Does it mean that if I have a directory (let's say /home/user1) with 3 
> subdirectories (/home/user1/mail, /home/user1/adm and /home/user1/prog ) , 
> I can define and export a fileset whose mount point is 
> "/.:/fs/home/user1/mail" and another fileset whose mount point is 
> "/.:/fs/home/user1", with the second fileset containing only the 'adm' and 
> 'prog' directories ? 

Yes, you can create a fileset and put two directories and a mount point
in any of its directories, including in its top-level directory. 

> If the answer is 'yes' , is it supported on every platform with LFS? Can 
> they be defined in any order ? 

This behavior is supported in all DCE DFS cache managers and in all DCE
DFS file (data) exporters.  I'm not sure exactly what you're getting at
with ``defined in any order'' but I believe that the answer is that yes,
you can set this up in any reasonable order.  That is, you can establish
the filesets in any order, complete with the mount point. 

		Craig 
 


