Subject: THE FUTURE OF OPEN SYSTEMS


The following is a summary of the "Experts Predict the Future of Open
Systems" panel discussion at UNIFORUM in San Francisco last month.  The
summary will appear in the forthcoming issue of the Network and Systems
Managers' Best Practices Report.  Comments are welcome.


The Future of Open Systems
A Panel of Experts Predicts the Top Trends in Open Systems for 1994 


	Nine industry experts gathered recently at UNIFORUM in a panel
discussion entitled "Experts Predict the Future of Open Systems." The
panel was chaired by Alan Paller, Director of Open Systems at Computer
Associates, and included representatives of vendors, consultants,
standards organizations, and the press.  Among the participants were Doug
Michels, Executive Vice President and Chief Technical Officer at SCO; Mike
DeFazio, Senior Vice President, UNIX System V, at Novell, Inc.; and Marshall
Goldberg, "Principal Evangelist" for Microsoft Corp.

The panel identified seven key trends shaping the Open Systems marketplace:

1.  UNIX systems vendors are seeing no growth in workstation--
UNIX on the desktop will be stagnant.  Over 60% of workstations 
sold in 1994 will be based on Pentium or high-end 486 
technology.  The only growth for UNIX workstations will be at 
the extreme high-end, with the SGI boxes used in advanced 
imaging applications.

2.  However, there is now, and will continue to be, enormous 
growth in UNIX servers (15-20%).  Indeed, growth that seems to 
be happening in workstations is actually happening in servers 
(for example, HP9000/700s classed as workstations are actually 
used as servers)  The UNIX server market is being driven by 
users' growing confidence that they can move their applications 
safely and cost-effectively to a UNIX platform.   Growth in 
servers is carrying the X-Terminals market (up 25%)--users will 
buy a lot of them for network and systems management.

3.  The database market will change, as database management 
systems become less expensive.  Microsoft's new SQL server-
based database is functional and cheap, and should have the 
effect of driving prices down across the market.  

4.  A shakeout in Application Development software has already 
begun.  With Powersoft and others introducing low-cost 
versions, higher priced products will slowly disappear.

5.  The biggest growth area in percentages will be systems and 
network management, as companies move their mission-critical 
applications down to open environments.  Systems and network 
management is commonly cited as the most significant barrier to 
moving to open systems.  Software is finally becoming available 
that is capable of managing heterogeneous environments, 
including truly distributed functionality, and users are 
spending freely to buy it.

6.  Systems Integrators willplay a huge role in helping 
companies move to open systems.  Faced with the daunting task 
of making the transition to open systems, users are looking 
increasingly to systems integrators.  At the same time, vendors 
have placed increasing emphasis on systems integrators as a 
channel for providing their technologies to end-users.

7.  COSE and OSF standards effort will have little impact on 
users, who will already have implemented various vendors' 
mostly-proprietary solutions.  Unfortunately, the goodwill of 
the standards groups will not be enough to overcome the net 
management vendors' economic interest in keeping their 
solutions as proprietary as possible.  Further, users need 
solutions today, and standards-compliant products are generally 
avaiable "in the future."  

8.  The buzzword of the year will be "object."  

Other Highlights:

Alan Paller provided his list of the "Things you're going to buy lots of."

*	Desktop computers and operating systems
*	Servers and operating systems
*	Applications
*	Database Management Systems
*	Application development tools
*	Systems management tools
*	Networks
*	Network management tools
*	Systems integration
*	Staff for operations and management
*	Training


Mike DeFazio described the developing open systems market from four 
perspectives:  technology, end-users, vendors, and channel.

*	The technology driver of the market, according to DeFazio, is the 
microprocessor. It's the enabler that allows improvements in processing, 
I/O, human interface, etc.  

*	The business users' imperatives for IT have changed:  in 1960s and
1970s, IT was used to decrease the cost of labor-intensive processes.  In
the late 1970s and 1980s, IT became tied more closely to protecting
revenues- -to helping companies differentiate themselves and maintain
their market position.  Today, IT is considered a revenue driver--helping
companies to be more effective. 

	The "virtual corporation" is emerging as IT moves from supporting 
internal functions to improving corporate processes.  Technology is being 
applied to a company's inputs from suppliers and its outputs to 
customers.  On the customer side, companies are trying to react quickly 
to customers' needs while maintianing economies of mass production.  The 
idea of "Mass Customization" based on fast time-to-market is emerging.

*	From the distribution perspective, the industry is moving from vertical
to horizontal integration.  It has gone from a few large companies
providing all components and finished products to a fully dis-integrated
industry, with many companies competing in discrete product/service areas. 
The channel is becoming more segmented, increasing competition and value
for end-users. 

*	From the supplier perspective, hardware and packaged software will be
lower value, service and support will be high-value by the later part of
the 1990s.  The solution will come from the channel (MIS is included in
the channel as a high-level solution provider).  The suppliers' duty will
be to provide quality products to the channel. 


Doug Michaels shared the results of a recent study by IDC confirming
Paller's assertion that while Windows dominates the desktop, host servers
(everything but the desktop) run predominantly UNIX, followed by Netware,
then OS2, with Windows NT representing a very small percentage of total of
servers shipped in 1993.  This trend is forecast to continue in the next
few years.  Thus, UNIX may not have a significant presence at the desktop
level, but it will remain dominant on the host server. 


...AND A REQUEST FOR YOUR HELP

The next issue of BPR will contain an article on security in open systems
and a comparitive analysis of IBM, HP, and Sun network management
platforms.  Because the newsletter relies on the experiences of actual
users, I'd greatly appreciate hearing from anyone who can provide insight
on either of these topics (your name and company will be kept confidential
unless you specify otherwise).  Specifically: 

SECURITY
-What commercial or public domain software are you using to implement 
security at your organization?  How is it working?  What are its strengths 
and weaknesses?
-What experiences have you had (good/bad) related to security that other 
managers could benefit from hearing about?
-What are the three most important factors for managers to consider in 
establishing a security policy

NETWORK MANAGEMENT PLATFORMS 
-What are the strengths and weaknesses of SunNet Manager, NetView/6000,
and OpenView? 
-What factors should managers weigh most heavily when deciding on a
network management platform? 

Again, your answers to these questions will be extremely valuable to us,
and I hope you'll take the time to respond.  If there is someone else in
your organization who might be better able to answer the questions, I'd
