In a recent discussion with an unnamed source within OSF, it became clear
that one of the biggest reasons for changing the development versus 
housekeeping funding model for OSF's technologies was to allow for 
far lower per-seat prices for DCE clients.  Specifically, if a company
that wishes to sell DCE and wants to pay a lot less per seat for
royalties, it can pay "up front" for DCE reference code development
instead.  Pay me now or pay me later ... I haven't actually seen a 
workup of the costs to see what the break-evens would be.  Any 
vendor types out there want to comment?

DCE is in a classic chicken-and-egg situation.  It'll get cheap when 
it's widely installed; it'll get widely installed when it's cheap.  The
new OSF setup is designed (partly) to break through this deadlock.

I mentioned to my source that several different current and prospective
DCE vendors gave me almost the same complaint -- "it (would cost) (costs)
about ($50) ($60) ($75) a copy for a DCE plus DFS client for (Mac) 
(Windows)" ... he smiled and said that under the new setup, it may be
possible for a vendor that will pay up front to cut that by as much as
an order of magnitude.  I can only hope....


