In <2nn1hb$so3@hpscit.sc.hp.com> sailer@a4430edc.esr.hp.com writes:
>The official definition of what is in COSE is sometimes called Spec 1170.

Spec 1170 was an effort started by OSF, USL, and (I think) some other
vendors to come up with a realistic definition of the Unix API.  It includes
basically all of what you think of as the Unix C library (for modern
versions of Unix).  It's called 1170 because that's how many functions
there are.

Nobody fully complied with all of the spec.  As I recall variances ranged
from three or four changes (OSF/1) to a couple-dozen (I forget who).  At
this point, I would expect all major Unix vendors to be have a slide that
shows there "1170 compliance."
