In article <2np4mf$7bh@paperboy.osf.org> rsalz@osf.org (Rich Salz) writes:
>	#include <pthread.h>
>
>	int cancelmode;
>
>	cancelmode = pthread_setcancel(CANCEL_OFF);
>	/* ...critical section here... */
>	if (cancelmode == CANCEL_ON)
>	    (void)pthread_setcancel(CANCEL_ON);

And be careful about what you do in the critical section. If the
non-cancelable thread gets blocked forever, your server looses
one of the call executor threads...

Also restoring the general cancelability can be done by just
calling:
	(void)pthread_setcancel(cancelmode);

I think, in general, the server manager routine should handle the
forwarded cancel by using TRY/CATCH exception handling, e.g.:

TRY
{
    /* Do your critical work with the other process. */
}
/*
 * If you want to handle the cancel specifically. */
CATCH(pthread_cancel_e)
{
    ...
}
 */
CATCH_ALL
{
    /* Do the necessary cleanup for the connection with the other process. */
}
ENDTRY

