Excerpts from netnews.comp.unix.osf.misc: 1-Apr-94 Re: Opinions sought
on AFS .. @campfire.sps.mot.com (1280) 

>    My understanding is that all the DFS users from the foreign cell share 
> one 'special unix id'.  So, if one foreign user creates 
> a file in the local cell, another foreign user from the same foreign cell 
> can access it because local DFS authenticates this new foreign user with the 
> same unix id. 

I believe that this isn't the case.  That is, it depends on what kind of
filesystem the file is stored under.  I believe also that there may be
some significant confusion here between how inter-cell trust
relationships are established and the actual authentication used. 

Second point first.  Indeed, two DCE cells have to share some
authentication information for one cell's credentials to be meaningful
in another cell, but this doesn't mean that all cross-cell
authentication devolves to sharing a single account. 

First point.  When a foreign user creates a file in a non-LFS system
(e.g. exported UFS), indeed the owner will be indistinguishable from the
anonymous user.  When the same foreign user creates a file in LFS (e.g.
Episode), though, their user&cell information is recorded and treated as
the file's owner for permission checks. 

This is latest belief, anyway; your mileage may vary, different vendors
are allowed to do different things; etc. 

		Craig 
 


