One of the motivations for providing a threads AES is portability.  Now that
vendors have begun to deploy DCE, applications are being developed.  It is
presumed that application developers may want to port their applications to
multiple platforms.  In this case, they would like the threads API to be
consistent across DCE implementations.  Without the AES, there is no
definition of what application writers can depend on being present on all
DCE platforms.  

Also, most vendors provide some measure of backward compatibility when
migrating to newer technologies.  Usually, customers are not happy to
discover that their applications won't work on their new system because of
incompatible improvements to the system.  Having a specification for the
existing threads architecture makes charting a migration course easier for
DCE vendors, application developers, and OSF.  Without a specification, it
isn't possible to ascertain the starting point for the migration.

-Arthur Harvey
 OSF


