In <2o126h$o7g@news.csie.nctu.edu.tw> hsiao@aho.cis.nctu.edu.tw (Ming-Chang Hsiao) writes:
> I am just curious whether DCE Threads is equal to P Threads or not ??

DCE uses and provides a threads package that is based on "draft 4" of the
posix threads document.  The threads standard was not finished at the the
time DCE needed it (it's not finished yet, either) so we decided to use
the then-current draft.  It is certainly OSF's stated goal to track
standards wherever relevent, and I would not be surprised if a future
release of DCE used and provided the official Posix standard, but I am
certainly not in a position to make any promises. :-)

The threads package that DCE uses/provides is documented in the Threads
Application Environment Specification, which will be available very soon
from OSF and (I think) X/Open.

>If DCE Threads is equal to P Threads, does it mean that
>P Threads program will be distributed computed among 
>machines running DCE.

I'm not sure I understand the question.  Yes, a client will be able to
spawn threads which each make indpendent RPC calls.  No, threads themselves
do not "migrate" across processors.  You probably meant the first.

>If I need the distributed computation function of threads 
>only, which components of DCE can be removed to save
>system resources ?

If you want to name your servers using file-lookup and string bindings
then you don't need GDS or CDS, the Global or Cell Directory Service.
If you don't want a true distributed filesystem then you don't need DFS.
If you don't need security then you don't need SEC.

Hope this helps.
	/r$


