probert@cuda.uhc.com (Paul Roberts) writes:

>I have recently been putting much thought into 
>naming conventions within a DCE application.
>Specifically, the naming of functions within  
>DCE to aid in maintaining the application and
>to foster re-use of the functions in other   
>applications.

...
>An example would be a server that defines an interface
>called "db". All DCE functions within that server 
>would have "db" as the prefix (i.e. db_load, db_insert,
>etc).

>I see as a positive with this convention in that I can
>tie a specific DCE function name to an interface. But   
>that is also a negative. What if after, or during, the
>design phase I decide to move that function out of that
>interface to a different one?

I don't see any of this as a big issue, or at least not one
that changes much with the introduction of RPC, as opposed
to ordinary procedures.  Function names should be chosen
to make the code readable.  If the module structure of the
code is well-designed, then including the module name in 
the function name will probably enhance the understanding of
the function.  Including the module name also reduces the
likelihood of a clash with a name exported by some other module. 
The same considerations apply if the module represents a
remote service.

I don't think that moving a function to another interface
is much of an issue.  First, I doubt that it will happen
very often. Second, I don't think that changing the name
of a function is very costly.  If you are still in the early
development phase, you just need to do some global find-and-replace
operations in the editor.  If you are beyond that point, you have
other considerations.  Remember that if you move the function,
you'll have to revise the IDLs of both interfaces and recompile
all affected servers and clients.  Any client that imported the
first interface but not the second will now have to also import
the second interface, and will have to consider how this changes
server binding.  If the clients programmers can put up with this,
then they can put up with renaming a function while they are at it.
-- 
