In <1994Mar30.200238.7653@newsgate.sps.mot.com> R1667C@email.sps.mot.com writes:
>   which was established during intercell setup).  Hence, if one
>   foreign user creates a file in the local cell, it is accessible by
>   another foreign user.  IBM says this shouldn't happen.  But it happened.

If all access is via DFS, as opposed to say telnet'ing to the server
then this is not an issue, right?

>6) There may be problems in migrating the existing software (applications as
>   opposed to files) to DFS.  For example, Oracle or Sybase servers need 
>   DFS authentication to write to the log files (or whatever) if they (log
>   files) are located in DFS.  

If the server runs as root, then the DCE library will automatically grant
them identity of the host where they are running.  (In DCE, there is a
unique principal for each host; this "machine identity" is maintained
by sec_clientd.)

>7) If the users filesystems (home directories) are in DFS,  they have
>   to authenticate to DCE to access their home directories and 
>   to authenticate to DCE they need to have a command prompt in their
>   home directory.  Some kind of catch 22.   

Not really.  The correct solution is for vendors to integrate dce_login
into their /bin/login program so that when you log in you authenticate
yourself to DCE, and the DCE security server tells your machine what your
"unix" identity is.  This integration will take some time; pressure your
vendors.

	/r$


