Summary: Press release about new book on DCE programming
Keywords: DCE application programming book


OSF DCE: Guide to Developing Distributed Applications by Harold W. Lockhart, 
Jr.; 561 pages; illustrated; 7-3/8" x 9-1/4; paperback with 3.5" disk included; 
$59.95; McGraw-Hill.  Publication: April, 1994.  ISBN: 0-07-911481-4

A treasure-trove of proven procedures and step-by-stap guidelines, OSF DCE by 
Harold W. Lockhart, Jr. focuses on essential aspects of Open Software 
Foundation (OSF) Distributed Computing Environment (DCE), telling how to use 
this standard to design and implement a state-of-the-art distributed system 
(McGraw-Hill, $59.95).

Since OSF DCE is rapidly being incorporated into distributed processing 
software in IBM, DEC, and UNIX environments, it has become a key working tool 
for application developers, systems analysts, and software engineers.  The 
author not only shows just what OSF DCE is and how it works, but also explains 
how it can best be used..and what problems it cannot solve.

In this up-to-the-minute work, Lockhart points out that OSF DCE provides a well-
integrated combination of components that provide high-level interfaces for 
developing distributed applications.  He analyzes twelve significant problems 
associated with distributed applications and demonstrates exactly how  OSF DCE 
can be used to solve, eliminate or minimize them.  He also presents four new 
client/server processing models with working programs, tiered servers, the 
single-threaded server, the multithreaded server, and the peer-to-peer server.

Subtitled Guide to Developing Distributed Applications, the book includes 
methods for utilizing Remote Procedure Call (RPC) tools and runtime facilities, 
examines the purpose and the capabilities of Security Service and how to invoke 
its features, and describes the optimal use of Directory and Time Services.  A 
3.5" disk that is packaged into the book contains practical application models 
in the C programming language with all source code, that can be used as a 
starting point for designing successful distributed applications.

Harold W. Lockhart, Jr., is a distributed systems design and development 
expert, with almost 20 years' experience in networking, software development, 
and engineering management.  He has been affiliated with such companies as Bell 
Atlantic, Westinghouse, Citicorp, Raytheon, Merck, and Grumman.


