That would be great.  I've been encouraged by what the DEC and OSF people
have said w.r.t. predictions of OSF/1's imminent demise (that is, such
rumours aren't true) but I remain worried that USL's inability to
completely destroy BSD-ish operating systems in court has been supplanted
by a more successful campaign for destroying them in the boardrooms of
Unix hardware/software producers and standards organizations.  Somebody
has to keep producing operating systems that meet my needs, and if it's
a little guy in Colorado ;) who can produce something that runs on
quality hardware (eg, SPARC, MIPS or Alpha) then so be it.

There's been a real problem lately with how vendors are viewing their
systems.  I can call up my Sun sales rep and tell him how things don't
work under Solaris 2.x, and he will laugh in my face and give me a
long list of applications I can buy that run under Solaris 2.x.  But
what I really want are not the big things working right, but the LITTLE
things that nobody makes much money off of.  I don't care if my operating
system supports Oracle or CAD apps or anything fancier than X11R5 and GCC.
But I care very much if the terminal emulation sucks, if the shell doesn't
work right, and if little tools are broken (eg, apropos) and require that
I waste my time working to get features I have taken for granted for
years, or changing how I work at the command line because somebody thinks
they should arbitrarily change the flags or arguments to some command
(to make it "better").  Those are real productivity thieves, IMO, and
irritating as hell.

At the same time, I can't really give vendors a compelling reason why
this is more important than their focus on big applications, from a
financial point of view.  I guess that makes me a "niche" customer.  I
certainly hope the burgeoning market for selling terminal-based dial-up
access will have some effect here.

