OK.  Off the top of my head, let's see...

(Please.  Shed light where you wish, but no flames, please.)

OSF was originally founded in response to fears that AT&T and SYN were
going to make Unix too proprietary.  HP, DEC, IBM, and some others got
together an created OSF, a not-for-profit organization where they could
collaborate on software without violating anti-trust laws.

Today, OSF is a success.  Many other large companies, including
SCO, SUN, and SGI, are now "members," which means they donate $$$
and creaturepower.  It "owns" Motif (X user interface
technology), OSF/1 (an implementation of SVR4 Unix), ANDF (an
architecture neutral software distribution technology, which
nobody seems to use but I don't know why not), and DCE and DME
(an rich set of programmer and system administrator tools for
building three-tiered client-server distributed systems).  OSF
has programmers who support all these products, and it generates
revenue by licensing these technologies to HP, IBM, DEC, SCO, and
recently SUN, and anybody else that wants to buy them.

Recently, a bunch of vendors got together and selected a subset of the
large array of possible technologies, such as (correct me where I'm
wrong) X, Motif, VUE (a Motif based look-and-feel from HP), NFS, TCP/IP,
and others and promised on a stack of copies of The White Book that all
the vendors would ship identical, interoperable user environments based
on these technologies.  This is called COSE (Common Open System
Environment?).  Now it looks like COSE will be "owned" by OSF.  The
official definition of what is in COSE is sometimes called Spec 1170.

Meanwhile, AT&T spawned USL, which owned the specification and an
implementation of Unix.  Then, last year, Novell bought USL.  Briefly,
Novell tried to claim that it was going to make Unix ***STANDARD*** by
proclaiming there version to be the one true version.  Reason prevailed,
so that Novell gave the *specification* of Unix (SVR4) to a group named
X/Open.  Novell keeps ownership of the USL Unix source code.  (Many
versions of Unix, such as HP-UX, SCO, and Solaris begin life as a
license to Novell/USL source code.  Vendors add features, perform their
own quality control, optimize for their hardware, etc.)  

X/Open is a different industry standards organization, also
comprising many companies large and small.  It tries to determine
the extent to which vendors adhere to the various standards.  For
example, HP-UX (and many others) is XPG4 branded, meaning that it
adheres to the X/Open definition of Unix.

X/Open and OSF are sort of converging, but they are still two quite
distinct organizations.

Of course, there are some unix vendors who do not use OSF code.  There
are some who don't bother with any of these standard setting
organizations.  In many cases these companies (usually small or
idiosyncratic) *do* in fact adhere to the standards, they just don't
play the political game (e.g., Linux, NEXTSTEP).

--
lee



